WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.384
Today’s lesson goal: Today’s lesson goal: declare typed variables in C sharp, read input and convert numbers safely, and calculate totals people can understand.

2
00:00:09.734 --> 00:00:23.774
Declare with a type: Declare with a type. String customer name equals Sarah. Int quantity equals 3. Double price equals 9.99. The type tells C sharp what kind of box you are creating.

3
00:00:24.124 --> 00:00:34.060
Why types help beginners: Types help beginners because the compiler catches many mistakes early. You see what each value is meant to be, and names plus types document your intent.

4
00:00:34.410 --> 00:00:43.074
Worked example: greeting: Worked example: a greeting. Write a prompt, read the line into a string name, then write Hello name with string interpolation.

5
00:00:43.424 --> 00:00:54.584
Worked example: tax total: Worked example: a tax total. Read a line of text, parse it with double.Parse, then multiply by one point two. Print the total with clear wording.

6
00:00:54.934 --> 00:01:05.254
Common mistakes: Common mistakes include using the value before Parse succeeds, forgetting that ReadLine returns text, and opening a single file instead of the whole project folder.

7
00:01:05.604 --> 00:01:18.948
Practice, then quiz: Practice, then quiz. Complete the Module 2 exercises in your console app, compare with solutions after you try, and take the Module 2 quiz. The written lesson has the full walkthrough.
